Members of the Welsh National Assembly Concern If They Find themselves at the Muppet Show
Some members of the National Assembly were shocked by the proposal that their title would be changed to the MWP (Member of the Welsh Parliament).
The alarm came after the National Assembly (Assembly's name) should rather be the Welsh Parliament (Welsh Parliament).
Representatives with different political backgrounds are now all fearing that the new title could make them the subject of ridicule.
According to one of the Labour MPs, the faction is concerned that their new name will rhyme with the words “Twp” and “Pwp”.
Not for the sake of our Welsh-readers: in Welsh, the twp means a simple one-street, and the pwp means pouch.
According to a representative of the Plaid party, the whole group is “unsatisfied” and therefore proposes alternatives.
One Conservative MEP said his group was “open” to the name change, however, he remarked, however, that there was only one leap from the MWP.
It’s worth noticing here that in the Welsh language the letter “w” is pronounced similarly to ‘u’ in English in yorkshire, i.e. the MWP etched.
The position of the National Assembly Committee responsible for drafting a draft law introducing a name change is as follows: “With regard to the titles used to naming the members of the National Assembly, the final decision is, of course, in the hands of the deputies.”
It was a 2017 law that ruled over the rule of Wales allowed the Welsh National Assembly to change its name.
In June, the Commission published the results of a public consultation promulgated by the House of Welsh Parliament's name, which received a wide-ranging subsidisation.
Concerning the address of Members, the Commission prefers Welsh Parliament Member (Member of the Welsh Computee) and the abbreviation of WMP, however, the public consultation supported the MWP alternative.
Deputies come up with newer and newer alternatives, but the agreement is rather cumbersome, which is a problem for the President of the National Assembly, Elin Jones, who will have to submit a bill on name changes in the coming weeks.
In addition, the reform-related law will affect the functioning of the National Assembly at other points, such as disqualification of deputies and committee structures.
MEPs will be able to vote their final vote on the issue in relation to their own title when discussing the draft law.
Macedonia holds a Refusal Referendum for Changing the Country’s Name
Citizens will vote on Sunday to change the country’s name to ‘North Macedonia’.
The aim of the referendum is to put an end to a decades-long debate with neighboring Greece, one of which is also called Macedonia.
Athens has long argued that its northern neighbour’s name essentially imposes a territorial claim and has repeatedly objected to its EU and NATO application.
Macedonian Prime Minister Gjorge Ivanov himself opposes a referendum on the name change and does not take part.
However, the supporters of the referendum, including Prime Minister Zoran Zaev, claim that the name change is simply a compromise that must be made for their accession to the EU and NATO.
