Macedonia holds a referendum to change the country's name
In Sunday’s Reflection Referendum, the people of Macedonia voted to change the country’s name “Nat-Macedonian Republic”. With this step they would resolve a decade-long disagreement with Greece, which prevented it from accepting the country’s request for EU and NATO membership.
“Greece, one of whose provincial provinces is called Macedonia, arguing that the name of his northern neighbor indicates that he wants a Greek territory of the same name, so he has vetoed the country’s membership in NATO and the EU so far.
The two-governments made a deal with the state’s new name in June, however, according to national-minded opponents, they would undermine the ethnic identity of Macedonia’s mostly Slavic population with the name change.
President Gjorge Ivanov has stated that he will not take part in the referendum, and as a result of the boycott campaign launched, it is doubtful whether there will be a 50% turnout required for a valid referendum.
“Would you support the country’s membership of NATO and the EU and will you accept an agreement with Greece?”
Proponents of the name-shioner, with Prime Minister Zoran Zaev at the forefront, are arguing that it is worthwhile to pay this price in order for Macedonia, as one of the countries that have risen in the ruins of Yugoslavia, to have a chance for EU and NATO membership.
“I have come today to vote for the country’s future so that the young people of Macedonia can live freely under the protection of the European Union, because it would be a safer life for all of us,” said Olivera Georgievska (79) in Skopje.
Although the referendum in itself is not legally binding, a sufficient member of the European Parliament has stated that they feel bound by the results of the vote.
A two-thirds majority is required in Parliament.
According to the State Electoral Electoral Commission, there were no reports of irregularities until 13:00 on the day of the vote.
The turnout, however, reached only 16% up to this point. This figure was 34% in the 2016 parliamentary elections (of which 66% of registered voters took part).
“For the sake of my children, I’m here to vote because it’s our place in Europe,” said Gjose Tanevski (62).
Zoran Zaev, Macedonia’s prime minister, his wife Zorica, and their son, Dushko, have voted in favour of the name change in Strumica in a referendum on September 30, 2018, which would allow the country to join the EU and NATO.
Vladimir Kavardarkov (54), in front of the parliament in Skopje, set up a small stage, chairs and tents for boycotts.
“We, too, support the country’s membership in NATO and the European Union, but we would like to join it proudly, with our heads raised, and not through the maid’s door,” Kavadarkov said.
“We’re a poor country, but we haven’t lost our dignity.
“If they don’t want to accept us as Macedonia, we can also turn to China or Russia as part of the Eurasian integration.”
According to Prime Minister Zaev, NATO membership could be counted on investments in Macedonia that a country with an unemployment of more than 20% is much needed.
“I believe that the majority will support our cause, because more than 80% of the country’s population basically supports EU and NATO membership,” Zaev said.
He also added that the “yes” vote “develposes to secure the country’s future”.
According to the results of a survey published last Monday by Macedonia’s political research institute last week, 30 to 43% of the voters are expected to take part in the referendum, which is lower than the required turnout.
According to another survey conducted by the Macedonian Telma TV, a 57% attendance is expected on Sunday.
Seventy-fifty percent of those who want to vote said they were going to vote yes.
In order for the Referendum to be valid, the turnout must be at least one vote more than 50%.
The invalidity of the Referendum would be the first serious blow to the pro-Western government, which came to power in May last year.
