The Labour Party will no longer support diversity.
Doesn’t tolerate those who are free to say what they think.
I wasn’t at all surprised when the activists of Momentum in my party decided to censor my censorship.
After all, I’m only the last in the long line of Labour MPs, with whom they were told that they weren’t welcome, simply because we’ve said what we’re thinking.
My fellow-mpreparator, Joan Ryan, received a similar treatment for steadily resisting anti-Semitism.
I didn't like the censor in my case that I didn't agree with Jeremy Corby.
This is the importance of a responsible economic policy on the issue of national security and Europe, which, ironically, exactly the issues in which Jeremy also did not agree with the former leaders.
Invitation to a Labour party meeting held in Nottingham East on Friday said they wanted an “inclusive-spirited and producing meeting.”
For most of my eight-year-long work as a member of the Workers’ Party, these Friday night’s reshuffles were exactly like this.
Today, however, the tone has shifted, and the promise of a “sufficient, more polite” policy has ceased to be forgotten, if it has ever been realised at all.
Increasingly, it is becoming more and more conspicuous that the Workers’ Party does not tolerate divergent views, and every opinion is judged on the basis of whether it is acceptable to the party’s leadership.
This trend began shortly after Jeremy became the leader of the couple. My co-workers with whom I thought we had a similar political stance have recently been waiting more and more lately to turn their backs on my individual opinion and to take a stand in favor of things I would otherwise never agree with, be it national security issues or a single European market.
“After all of my public sermon, no matter what I’ve actually said, they’re starting to blaspheme on social media, they’re demanding my replacement, criticizing the politics in the middle, telling me that I shouldn’t be a member of the Workers’ Party.
It doesn't just happen to me.
Moreover, I do know that I’m more fortunate than a lot of my colleagues, because the comments to me are usually confined to politics.
“I am astonished by the professionalism and resentment of my political colleagues who are compelled to endure sexist and racist attacks on a daily basis, but they do not run.
The most disgracing aspect of today's politics is that attacks at this level have become the norm.
“Last week, Jeremy Corbyn said that the Labour Party should support a culture of tolerance.
The reality, however, is that we renounced diversity, and with every “confidence of confidence” and every amendment of the electoral rules, the circle of people tolerated in the party is narrowing.
Over the past few years, a lot of people have advised me to restrain myself, let’s not be so eviscerating, and then “everything will be fine”.
That’s not why I’ve taken a political career.
Thirty-two years ago, as a student, I joined the Workers’ Party because I was outraged that the Thatcher-government so neglected the schools that our classroom almost crumbled, and then I was fighting for a better near-smooth, for the sake of those who were the most in need, both local counsellor and government minister.
“I’ve never hidden my political views, and I didn’t do it differently in the last election.
It was very clear for everyone in Nottingham East what political positions I took and on which issues I disagree with the current leadership.
For those of those who supported the motion on Friday, I would like to say that in this situation, when the country is moving towards a Brexit that will harm not only families but also companies and public services, I don’t understand why we have to spend so much time and energy into questioning my loyalty to the leader of the Labour Party.
My most important message, however, goes not to the members of the Nottingham Momentum, but to my constituents, regardless of whether they are members of the Workers’ Party or not: I’m proud that I can serve you, and I promise that no threat of deviatory or for political purposes will deter me from acting in my own judgment and in view of everyone’s interests.
Chris Leslie, representative of Nottingham East
