“The sea is ours”: Bolívia, who does not have a beach, hopes the court will re-launch the path to the Pacific.
Sailors patrol the sailed nautical headquarters in La Paz.
On public buildings, ocean blue flags are wreaking.
The naval bases from Lake Titicaca to theAmazon proclaim the motto: “We have a boat to the sea.
“It’s our duty to get it back.”
Bolivia, which does not have a coast, still vigorously lives the memories of the lost shore, taken by Chile in a 19th-sts-throomed blood-stricken conflict of resources, and there is a strong desire to re-select the waves of the Pacific.
These hopes may have prevailed for many decades, as Bolivia is awaiting enforcing an enshrined five-year deliberation by the International Court of Justice on 1 October.
“Bolívia has the ball that floods people with the spirits of togetherness and peace, who are waiting for the outcome in a positive mood,” said Roberto Calzadilla, a Bolivian diplomat.
A lot of Bolivians monitor the decision of the International Court of Justice on projections, hoping to stand up for Bolivia in The Hague’s judiciary, and after several decades of rhapso-speaking attitude, it will be obligatory to sit at the negotiating table so that Bolivia can have independent access to the sea.
Evo Morales, the charismatic and indigenous president of Bolivia, who is facing a contradictory struggle to continue for next year’s re-election, could lose a lot with Monday’s verdict.
“We are very close to coming back to the Pacific,” he said at the end of August.
However, some analysts believe that the judiciary is unlikely to decide in favor of Bolivia, and it wouldn’t even change the situation if its decision were favorable for Bolivia.
The U.N. body in the Netherlands does not have the power to judging Chilean areas and has also stipulated pre-termination that it will not affect the outcome of the possible negotiations.
“The International Court of Justice’s verdict just six months after the conciliations indicates that the case was “not complicated,” said Paz Zárate, an expert on international law in Chile.
Moreover, the past four years have not only not helped the cause of Bolivia, but it has even retracted it.
“The current Bolivian government has diverted the issue of the maritime exit for its own purposes,” Zárate said.
“Because of Morales’ hostile rhetoric, the residual benevolence of the Chileans also faded.”
At some point, Bolivia and Chile will certainly continue the negotiations, but it will be extremely difficult to initiate a dialogue after what has happened.
Since 1962, the two countries have not delegated an ambassador to each other.
Former president Eduardo Rodriguez Veltzé, a representative of Bolivia in The Hague, does not agree with the conclusion that the court’s decision-making was unusually rapid.
“Chanminglie on Monday is an “emergent opportunity to open a new chapter in relations with Chile,” he said, with a chance to “throat from 139 years of strife,” he said.
Calzadilla has also denied that Morales, still one of Latin America’s most popular presidents, used the naval debate to take a political advantage.
“Bolivia will never relinquish its rights to the Pacific,” he said.
Judging gives us an opportunity to see that it’s time to go beyond the past.”
