Huge head lids appeared at the Paris fashion shows, which will soon appear in high-end stores.
If you would like to expand your hat collection, or you want to completely cover the day, you don’t have to look any further.
Valentino and Thom Browne unveiled an unusually oversized headgear as part of their spring/summer 2019 collection, greeted with great enthusiasm by the stylish audience of the Paris Fashion Week.
Unplantic hats aroused sensations on Instagram during the summer, and the above fashion designers also put their eye-catching works on catwalks.
Valentino’s most spectacular piece was an exaggerated beige hat, decorated with feather-like, wide flange, and almost completely absorbed the models’ heads.
Among the other oversized accessory, gemed watermelon, a sorcerer’s hat and even a pineapple that weren’t really designed to keep one’s head warm.
Thom Browne also came up with a selection of bizarre masquerading masks just before Halloween.
Many of the colored masks had their mouths sewn together, reminded me more of Hannibal Lecter rather than haute couture.
One of them resembled a dive masquera (coded with pipes and glasses), and another looked like a melt-breasted funnel.
If you like these spectacular pieces, you’ll be lucky.
According to styling analyzers, it is possible that the huge hats are already on their way to high-end clothing stores.
The over-sized hats follow the path trodged by the “La Bomb”, a 60-centimeter-width-wide straw clay clay clay cabbage that we’ve already seen from Rihanna to Emily Ratajkowski.
The cult brand behind the less practical hat that conquered social networking sites presented another piece on the runway: a beach bag made of straw, which is almost as large as the swimsuit model holding it.
The dark-denominated rafia sack, decorated with rafia witches and on the top, was an outstanding piece of Jacquemus’ Spring/Summer’s Spring/Summer La Riviera collection in Paris.
The stylist Luke Armitage told FEMAIL: “I’m counting that huge hats and beach bags will appear in high-end clothing stores next summer. The designer has had such an elemental effect from everyone that it’s hard to deny the demand for oversized accessories.”
“Plaublish-Knicourse is Essential for Global Citizens.”
Scottish independent schools have traditionally provided an excellent education. Considering new outstanding exam results, this situation has not changed until 2018, which will only be reinforced by individual and group successes in sports, arts, music and other community initiatives.
More than 30,000 students in Scotland are already educated in institutions of this type, represented by The Scottish Council of Independent Schools (SCIS), which striving to provide students and parents with the best possible services.
Non-independent schools strive to prepare students for further education, tertiary education, for their chosen careers and the position they occupy as citizens of the world.
In an education sector where it is possible to design and introduce customized school curricula, we can see that modern languages continue to prove to be popular and sought-after subjects.
Nelson Mandela said, “If you talk to someone in a language that you understand, it comes to your head.
“If you speak in his tongue, it will reach his heart.”
“It’s an important reminder because we can’t relily rely solely on English if you want to build liaisons and trust from other countries.
Based on this year’s exam results, we can see that the students of independent schools brought the best results from foreign languages all over the country.
Sixty-six per cent of all students who learned a foreign language achieved a higher grade (more than 85 per cent).
According to the data collected from 74 SCIS member schools, 72 per cent of the students received a higher degree in tangerine, while 72 per cent of students in German, 69, and 63% of Spaniards in Spaniards and 63% of Spaniards.
These achievements also show that the independent schools in Scotland regard foreign languages as essential knowledge, the knowledge of which children and young people will undoubtedly need in the future.
Today, the languages, as chosen subjects, have become at least as important as natural sciences, technology, technical sciences, and mathematics, both in independent and other schools.
A 2014 survey conducted by the United Kingdom’s Employment and Vocational Training Committee showed that 17 per cent of employers found that filling vacancies is difficult due to a lack of language proficiency.
From this we can also see that language proficiency is becoming more and more necessary in order for young people to have a successful career.
With more and more people waiting for the knowledge of foreign languages, language proficiency is essential in a globalized world.
Irrespective of what kind of career a person chooses when he/she learns a foreign language, he gains a life-threatening knowledge that has a clear advantage.
The ability to communicate directly with other nationalities automatically provides a competitive advantage for multi-language candidates.
According to a YouGov survey of 4,000 adults in the UK in 2013, 75 per cent of the respondents didn’t be able to speak well enough in a foreign language to talk to others, and French was the only language spoken by more than 10% (15 percent of respondents).
Therefore, it is important to put an Emphasis on children’s language teaching.
Talking in several languages, especially in the languages of developing countries, can be a tool in the hands of children who, equipped to be equipped, have a better chance to apply for more important jobs.
Each school in Scotland will deal with a variety of languages.
Numerous schools will focus on classical modern languages, while some will teach languages, such as Mandarin or Japanese, which are expected to be of paramount importance for the United Kingdom by 2020.
“No matter what your child’s area of interest, you’ll be able to learn a number of elective languages for independent schools and will be trained by educators who are experts in their field.
Scottish-independent schools are committed to creating a learning environment for children that prepares and equips them with the skills they will need to be successful, save for the future.
“It’s not difficult to notice that in today’s global business environment, languages continue to be vital for a country’s future, and that education needs to reflect.
As a matter of fact, knowledge of modern languages should rather be treated as an “international communication skills.”
Responsible schools offer opportunities for youngsters of Scotland to choice, diverse and quality education.
Il faut bien le faire – that’s the way it has to be.
John Edward, Chairman of the Scottish Council of Independent Schools
