“There’s no way” North Korea unilaterally dismantled if you don’t trust your negotiator
North Korea’s foreign minister told the U.N. on Saturday that maintaining the sanctions only increased mistrust of the United States, and there’s no way the country could unilaterally renounce its nuclear arsenal.
Ri Jong-ho said North Korea took “significant and genealogical steps” last year, such as halting nuclear and rocket tests, shutting down the site of nuclear testing, and promising not to expand its nuclear arsenal and the scope of nuclear technology.
“Unfortunately, we don’t see a similar response from Americans,” he said.
“Unconfidence in America, we feel that the security of our nation is unrestricted, and under these circumstances there is no non-respiratory and first dismantled.”
Although Ri reiterated North Korea's frustratedness that Washington is resisting the idea of “sea” nuclear disarmament, which, according to a scenario, North Korea would be rewarded for the actions taken so far, its statement seemed significant because it did not clearly reject unilateral nuclear disarmament, as Pyongyang has done so far.
Ri cited a joint statement made by Kim Jong-un and Donald Trump at the first ever summit between a ruling U.S. president and a North Korean leader that took place in Singapore on June 12, during which Kim had pledged to “nuclear dismantle the Korean peninsula,” while Trump guaranteed the integrity of North Korea.
North Korea is striving to formally put an end to the 1950-53 Korean War, but the U.S. believes Pyongyang must first give up its nuclear weapons.
D.D. did not give up on strict international sanctions against North Korea.
“America insists we start with nuclear disarmament and impose sanctions on the pressure to force its will on us, and it also protests against declaring an end to the war,” Ri said.
“The notion that we can be brought to their knees with sanctions is a fantasy for those who don’t know us.
“The problem is that unrelieving sanctions only increase our suspicion.”
Ri didn't mention planning a second summit between Kim and Trump, which the U.S. president specifically spoke at the United Nations earlier this week.
The minister emphasized that there have been three meetings between Kim and South Korean President Moon Jaein over the past five months, he added: “If the nuclear disarmament-afsued country were South Korea, not the U.S., the nuclear disarmament of the Korean peninsula wouldn’t have made such a stalemate.”
Nonetheless, the tone of Ri’s speech was dramatically different from last year, when he told the United Nations General Assembly that targeting the North-American continent with North Korean rockets is inevitable after Trump, the “vicious president,” called Kim, a “rocket man,” who was on a suicidal voyage.
At the United Nations this year, Trump – who threatened North Korea with “complete devadication” last year – praised Kim for having the courage to take steps toward decommissioning, but he also noted that there’s still a lot of work to be done and the sanctions will remain until North Korea enforces nuclear disarmament.
“On Wednesday, Trump said he didn’t set a time limit, meaning it wouldn’t matter if it took two years or two years, it didn’t matter.”
“Ukraine and Russia are arguing that the U.N. Security Council could reward Pyongyang for what we’ve taken so far.”
U.S. Secretary of State Mike Pompeo said at the U.S. Security Council on Thursday: “Safe Security Council Sanctions must be steadily enforced until we have achieved the actual, final, probable nuclear disarmament.”
Since 2006, the Security Council has unequivocally maintained sanctions against North Korea in order to curb Pyongyang’s nuclear and ballistic missile programs.
Pompeo met with Rí during a pause by the United Nations General Assembly and then said he would travel back to Pyong Pyongyang next month to prepare for the second summit.
Pompeo visited North Korea three times this year, but his last trip didn't go very well.
When he left Pyongyang in July, he announced that progress had been made, but North Korea had accused him hours later of proclamation with “gear-like demands.”
As part of a meeting with Moon this month, North Korea has pledged to dismantle a missile base and a nuclear facility if the U.S. takes “the necessary steps.”
“The “suffituating steps” mentioned by Kim would be security guarantees that Trump promised in Singapore and actions that could lead to normalizing the relationship with Washington.
Journalists at Harvard are instructed to relax enough.
A new subject at Harvard University this year prompted the students to get more sleep, thus fighting against the growing macho culture of caffeine-fuelled full-day-waiting.
An academist has come to the conclusion that the students of the world’s number one university are often completely lost when it’s a matter of taking care of themselves.
According to him, the first of its kind of training in the U.S. was designed by Professor Charles Czeisler, who teaches sleep medicine at Harvard School of Medicine and practicing as a specialist at Brigham and Women’s Hospital.
He was encouraged to develop the course by giving a lecture earlier on the impact of sleep deprivation of learning.
“In the end, a little girl came up to me and said, ‘Whether I’m only faced with this as a senior?’
“He’s never told him about the importance of sleep, which surprised him,” he told the Telegraph.
The course, launched for the first time this year, students will be informed about the fundamental effects of adequate sleeping habits on their academic and athletic performance, in addition to having a positive impact on their general well-being.
According to Paul Barreira, psychiatrist professor at the Harvard School of Medicine and director of the university’s health service, the university’s decision was to start the course because the students were found to be extremely sleep-depleted during the week.
The one-hour course also includes a series of interactive tasks.
In one of them, for example, they show a picture of a dormitory room where students can click on coffee cups, curtains, sneakers and books to get acquainted with the effects of caffeine and the effects of light, how sports performance is affected by sleep deprivation, and why the bedtime routine is important.
Participants are told in another section how long-distance sleep deprivation raises the risk of heart attack, stroke, depression and cancer.
Introducing a map of the university’s area with interactive icons to encourage the participants to rethink their daily routine.
“We know we won’t immediately change students’ behavior.
“But we believe that they have the right to this knowledge – just as they have the right to be aware of the health effects of smoking,” Czeisler said.
There is still flourishing a culture in which it counts as an accomplishment to learn a night, he said, adding that modern technology and the ever-increasing pressure on students result in endless deprivibility causing ever-increasing problems.
Sufficult and high-quality sleep is a “secret weapon” for students to combat stress, depletion and anxieties, and even from obesity, because sleep deprivation forces the brain into starving mode and causes a constant sense of hunger.
Raymond So helped Professor Czeisler develop the course, a 19-year-old student from California studying chemistry and biophysique, and last year he took one of the professor’s courses as a freshman at Harvard.
He said that what he heard there opened his eyes and urged him to start a university-level training.
He hopes that as the next step, post-graduate students will also be able to participate in a similar degree program before finding a job in the private sector.
Professor Czeisler has recommended that the students set a warning for bedtime, wake up for waking, and familiarize themselves with the harmful effects of the “blue light” emitted by electrical screens and LED light sources, which can easily get them out of their daily rhythm and make it difficult to fall asleep.
