HIV/AIDS: The number of new patients increased by 14% in China
China has announced a 14% increase in the number of citizens living with HIV and AIDS in the country.
According to health officials, this is more than 820,000 affected.
In the second quarter of 2018 alone, the number of reported cases increased by about 40,000.
A significant majority of those newly infected sexually acquiring the disease.
Previously, the disease was spread rapidly by transmigration of blood in some parts of China.
At a conference in Yunnan, however, health officials stated that the number of such incidents has since fallen to nearly zero.
However, compared to the previous year, the number of people living with HIV and AIDS in China increased by 100,000.
Breast-sufficient transmission of HIV poses a serious problem for the Chinese LGBT community.
Homosexuality has not been a crime in China since 1997, but discrimination against the community has been very strong ever since.
According to the estimates published in various studies, 70-90% of men having sexual relations with other men due to the country’s Conservative value system eventually marry a woman.
In many cases, the disease in these relationships is transmitted due to inadequate sexual protection.
Since 2003, the Chinese government has provided universal access to anti-HIV drugs as part of a program aimed at resolving the problem.
