Inventor of the World’s Wide Web would launch a new internet to defy Google and Facebook
Tim Berners-Lee, the inventor of the World Wide Web, is launching a startup to compete with Facebook, Amazon and Google.
The most recent project of the technological legend, Inrupt, is a company based on Berners-Lee’s open-source platform, Solid.
With Solid, users will be able to choose where they want to store their data and who will have access to individual information.
Berners-Lee joked in an exclusive interview with the Fast Company that the intention behind Inrupt is “acquivalence of control over the world.”
“We have to do it right now,” he said.
“It’s a historic moment.”
The application uses Solid technology to enable people to create their own “personal online repository”, also known as POD.
This may include lists, to-to-peak, calendar, music library, as well as other personal and professional tools.
It’s just like Google Drive, Microsoft Outlook, Slack, and Spotify available from the same browser at the same time.
In the personal online repository, it’s unique that the user can decide in its entirety who has access to his/her data.
The company deems this as “appropriate the right of self-determination of persons to self-determination over data”.
According to John Bruce, CEO of Inrupt, the company’s task is to provide resources, processes, and adequate skills to make Solid accessible to everyone.
The company currently consists of Berners-Lee, Bruce, an IBM-accompanied security platform, some contract developers working for the project and a community of volunteer programmers.
As of this week, technology developers around the world will be able to create their own decentralized applications by using the tools available on the Inrupt page.
Berners-Lee says that he and his team do not ask “Facebook and Google whether to introduce a full-fashionable technology that would turn the company’s business models on its entirety on its heads overnight.”
“We don’t ask them permission.”
Berners-Lee wrote in an Instagram post published by Medium on Saturday that “Inrupt’s “situation to provide commercial energy and an ecosystem that will help protect the integrity and quality of the newly built-based network on Solid.”
Berners-Lee changed the Internet in 1994 when he founded the World Wide Web Consortium at the Technical University of Massachusetts.
Berners-Lee has participated as an important opinion-scientist in recent months in disputes surrounding the neutrality of the Internet.
Berners-Lee will remain the founder and director of the World Wide Web Consortium, the Web Foundation and the Open Data Institute.
“I am undeclaredly optimistic about the next epoch of the Internet,” Berners-Lee said.
