The National Trust is searching for the secret life of bats.
New research is being carried out on a Scottish-Greek estate to find out how bats use the characteristics of the landscape during hunting.
Scientists hope that the results will provide you with new information on the behavior of unique flying mammals, which could benefit from future conservation initiatives.
Scientists from the National Trust for Scotland will look at the behavior of ordinary and soprano dwarf pebs, as well as brown long-denominated bats and aquatic bat in the Inverewe Garden in Wester Ross.
Special recording devices will be placed at the relevant points of the area so that they can record the activities of the bats during the season.
The NHS employees and their volunteers will carry out mobile data collection with manual detectors.
By analyzing the audio material of the recordings, you can determine the frequency of the bat sounds and what each species is doing.
Scientists also create a habitat map and a report, which allows us to determine in detail how the behavior of the animals develops depending on the landscape.
Rob Dewar, an NTS conservation adviser, hopes that the results will reveal which habitats are the most important habitats for bats and how each species uses them.
This information will help to determine the beneficial effects of habitat management works, such as meadow design, and how best to preserve forested areas for bats and other affected species.
In the territory of Scotland and the United Kingdom, there has been a significant decrease in the size of bat populations over the last century.
The construction and regional development works disturb the sleeping places of the animals and reduce their living space.
Wind turbines and public lighting are both a risk factor for them, just as they are placed flypapers, certain chemical processes used for building materials, as well as attacks by domestic cats.
Bats aren't exactly blind.
However, because of their mast predatory nature, they rely much more on their hearing when they hunt for pretentious rather than on their sight.
Their sophisticated technique based on ultrasophal revermance can determine the exact position of insects as well as the landmarks in their journey.
NTS, responsible for maintaining more than 270 historic buildings, 38 gardens and 76,000 hectares of land across the country, takes the issue of bats very seriously.
They work with ten skilled specialists, who regularly carry out studies and sleeping inspections, as well as occasionally taking part in rescue efforts.
Scotland’s “precise its first and only dedicated bat rupture” has been established on the Threave estate in Dumfries and Galloway, home of eight of Scotland’s ten bats.
According to estate manager David Thompson, the area is an ideal habitat for bats.
“Threave has a great place for the bats,” he said.
“There are old buildings, there are a lot of old trees, and everything else necessary for a good living space.
“But we don’t yet know a lot about the bats, and thanks to the work done here and in other similar fields, we can find out more about what they need to feel great.”
He also emphasized how important it is to make it possible to make sure before the renovation of the buildings whether bats live there, as the destruction of a single settlement could result in the destruction of 400 females and juveniles, which could lead to the extinction of the entire local population.
Bats are protected animals, their destruction, harassment and harassing habitats are prohibited by the law.
Elisabeth Ferrell, a representative of the Bat Conservation Trust in Scotland, also called for help from the population.
“There’s still a lot we don’t know about bats, and we don’t have almost no information about the situation in the population of most domestic species.”
