He added: “It’s “concercerned” that if the United Kingdom leaves the EU unaltered unaltered, the automaker would be forced to temporarily stop production at its factory in Burnaston near Derby.
“We need an agreement,” Clark said.
According to the Japanese auto-manufacturing company, in the event of a no-deal Brexit, jobs could be lost due to delays at the borders.
Last year, the production of the Toyota Auris and Avensis models produced 150,000 cars, 90% of which were exported to other Member States of the European Union last year.
“The way I can see that if the United Kingdom is eliminated from the EU at the end of March, there will be shutdowns at the factory,” said Marvin Cooke, Managing Director of Toyota in Burnaston.
Other car manufacturers operating in the United Kingdom (including Honda, BMW and Jaguar Land Rover) were also concerned that the country would leave the EU without an agreement on cross-border commerce.
For example, BMW has stated that it will be shutting down its Oxford-manufactured plant for a period of one month after Brexit.
Car manufacturers are most concerned that there are risks associated with the supply chain in the event of a no-deal Brexit.
The Toyota production line operates on an “on-time” basis, with parts arriving every 37 minutes from suppliers operating in the UK and other EU Member States.
If the United Kingdom withdrew from the European Union without agreement on 29 March, intermittent disruptions can lead to production delays and lack of spare parts according to industry representatives.
According to the Toyota, it would be impossible to store more than one-past of more than one-pasturdy set of spare parts, so the production would have to be halted.
Clark said his plan, known as Theresa May Chequers, for future ties with the European Union, is “extrely compiled in such a way as to avoid delayed checks at the border.”
