The head of the Ministry of Health sent a letter to the country’s gratitude to the staff of the NHS from the countries of the European Union, and emphasized that he hopes that they will still stay after Brexit.
Jeane Freeman, a member of the Scottish Parliament, sent the letter less than six months before the United Kingdom’s expected withdrawal.
The Scottish Government has already agreed to cover the costs associated with settled career applications for EU citizens working in a decentralised public service.
Freeman wrote: “During the course of the summer, the exit negotiations between the United Kingdom and the European Union have continued, as a result of which a decision is expected to be made in the autumn.
The United Kingdom government has also embarked on the preparations for a non-contraciliation scenario.
“I am aware of the disturbing period that may be for you.
That’s why I would like to reiterate reiterating the efforts of every member of the team, no matter what country they come from.
Employees from different EU Member States and from other EU countries will provide valuable experience and expertise for the work of the Ministry of Health, which also has a positive impact on patients and our communities.
“Scotland is fully your home, and we really want them to stay with us.”
