Foreign buyers will have to pay a higher levy on real estate purchased in the United Kingdom
According to Toryk’s new plan, foreign buyers will have to pay a higher levy for real estate purchased in the United Kingdom, and they would support the homeless.
With this move to neutralize Corbyn’s success among young voters
Elevating the levy is aimed at those who are not taxed in the United Kingdom
According to the state treasury’s calculations, this would generate an annual revenue of 120 million pounds, which would be used to support the homeless.
Foreign buyers will have to pay a higher levy on real estate purchased in the United Kingdom, and they will spend the additional revenue from it to supporting the homeless, Theresa May is scheduled to announce at a press conference today.
The aim of the move is likely to counter the success of Jeremy Corbyn among young voters by promising more affordable housing and high-income measures.
An increase in levies will affect individuals and companies who are not taxed in the United Kingdom, and the additional revenue received will be used to combat homelessness.
The additional fee added to the current levy, in addition to the higher levy imposed two years ago on second flats and real estate purchased for spending purposes, can reach up to three per cent.
The Treasury expects an annual revenue of GBP 120 million.
It is estimated that 13% of newly-contained real estate in London will be purchased by non-UK citizens, causing a significant rise in prices, which makes the situation of first home buyers more and more difficulty.
Many of the country’s rich districts have become “ghost towns”, especially in the capital, as there are a lot of foreign buyers who spend most of their time outside the country.
The new bill came just a few weeks after Boris Johnson called for a reduction in levies to help young people preparing to wait for their first home.
Personally, he blames large construction corporations responsible for keeping real estate prices at a high level, for whom it has become the custom to buy the lands and to leave a waste. He urged May to abolish the quotas on affordable houses, and to do something with a “victory market situation in the country”.
Corbyn has announced remarkable suggestions for a series of Housing reforms, including rent control and the abolition of unreasonable evictions.
In addition, it would provide more powers and opportunities for advice for the construction of new houses.
“Last year I said: “Last year I’d say that my prime minister’s prime minister’s office wanted to dedicate it to the restoration of an English dream and to make life better for each generation.
To do this, it is necessary to deal with the real estate market situation.
Britain, as always, is still open to those who want to live, to work and to start a family.
However, it’s inappropriate that private individuals living outside the country, as well as foreign-emecreed corporations, can buy real estate just as easily as hard-working UK citizens.
“Draising from your own home has become almost unattainable for too many, and the unworthy state of homelessness has become too unattainable.”
Jack Ross: “I want to be the manager of the Scottish team.”
"Sunderland coach Jack Ross said his "suppliance" was to become the manager of the Scottish national team.
The 42-year-old Scottish coach would be happy to take on the challenges of resurrecting the North-East club, which is currently in third place in the first division league, just three points behind the first-team.
