“When I was a kid, I’ve been watching the Scottish team’s game in Hampden with my father, and I was always pulling me back.
“The only way I have a chance now is to be successful as a club manager.”
Ross’ predecessors include Dick Advocaat, David Moyes, Sam Allardyce, Martin O’Neill, Roy Keane, Gus Poyet and Paulo Di Canio.
Former Alloa Athletic coach is not worried that he will have to follow in the footsteps of such big names and had previously declined the offer of Barnsley and Ipswich Town.
“At the moment, I’m going to see if I can get the team back in the first division.
“If you look at the club’s structure and its facilities, it’s undoubtedly the first-to-tier league,” he said.
“It won’t be easy to get back, but it’s more likely that I’m going to consider my activity here’s success only if we can get the club back.”
Ross’s managerial career began only three years ago, after serving as a deputy coach with Dumbarton for a while and spent 15 months in the Hearts coaching team.
He then assisted Aolla recover after the team returned to the third tier, scoring the league title with St. Miraren on the verge of relegation in the following season.
According to Ross, he feels more self-confessed now now than he has ever been in his playing career, such as Clyde, Hartlepool, Falkirk, St. Mirren or Hamilton Academical.
“It’s been a very defining moment, he recalled the period he spent as the coach of Alloa.
“Obviously I’d think management was better for me than to be a player.
That sounds strange, because I did a good job, I've had a very good life, and I've even had some really reminiscent moments.
The lives of the players are sometimes very tough.
There's a lot you have to go through every week.
Of course, I still have to cope with the stress and pressure of work, but the management tasks lie for me.
“I’ve always been interested in management, and now that I’ve tasted it, I feel the best in my skin when I’ve been an adult.”
