According to a new study, the beer feels the most at 5:30 a.m. on Saturday.
In the summer heatwave, the turnover of pubs in England has increased significantly, but restaurant chains are still under a lot of pressure.
According to the collected data, the revenues of pubs and bars increased by 2.7% in July, but that of restaurants declined by 4.8%.
Peter Martin, an employee of the business consulting firm CGA, who compiled the statistic, commented: “Thanks to the sunny period and its longer-than-than-expected World Cup appearance, the trends of the previous month continued in July, when pubs experienced an increase of 2.8% in revenue, with an even greater decline in restaurants.
Revenue reductions of 1.8% of restaurants in June worsened in July.
The pubs and bars selling mostly alcohol performed best, who showed an even greater increase even higher than the loss at restaurants.
Pubs offering more food were also embarrassed by the long-lasting sunshine, although not nearly as dramatically as the operators of restaurants.
Looks like people are more in the mood to drink at this time.
Revenues from beverages of pubs and bars operated by companies increased by 6.6% this month and food revenues fell by 3%.”
According to Paul Newman, an RSM analyst for leisure and catering: “These results follow the trend that has emerged since the end of April.
The weather, as well as major community and sporting events, will continue to be the main source of income in the entertainment and hospitality industry.
Unsurprisingly, restaurant chains are in a difficult situation, and an annual revenue loss of 4.8% can become particularly painful due to constant cost pressure.
“It couldn’t have come at a bad time for restaurant operators, and it won’t come to any time, and it’ll only be over time to see if the more moderate weather experienced in August will bring a nimity in this area as well.”
The total revenue growth of pubs and restaurants was 2.7% in July, which also reflects the slowing rate of the introduction of new brands.
The Coffer Peach Tracker, which monitors sales of the UK hospitality sector (pubs, bars and restaurants) and is a widely-accepted industry-based measure, collects and analyses the performance data of an operating group with a total turnover of more than 9 billion pound, with a total turnover of more than 9 billion pounds.
