2004: On December 26, a massive sea eruption began on the west coast of northern Sumatra’s Aceh province that sparked a tsunami that had reached 14 countries, which claimed a total of 226,000 lives along the Indian Ocean coastline, with more than half of the casualties being killed in Aceh.
2005: Serious quakes shook the western coast of Sumatra between the end of March and the beginning of April.
Hundreds of people were killed on the island of Nias near Sumatra.
2006: A 6.8 magnitude quake shook the southern part of Java, Indonesia’s largest island, and the tsunami claimed almost 700 lives on the southern shore.
2009: 7.6 earthquake devastated Padang, the capital city of Western Sumatra.
More than 1,100 people lost their lives.
2010: A 7.5 quake shook an island of the Mentawa archipelago near Sumatra and in some places triggered a 10-metre tsunami that destroyed dozens of villages and killed about 300 people.
