North Korea's dissidents "decreased" under Kim
The number of North Korean dissidents headed to South Korea has declined since Kim Jong-un came to power seven years ago, a South Korean legislature said.
Park Bjengszok cited data from the Ministry of Unification of the South, and said that in the last year there were 1,727 dissidents compared to 2011, when this number was 2,706.
Park said that closer border surveillance between North Korea and China, as well as higher penalties imposed on human smugglers were the main reasons.
Pyongyang did not comment on this information publicly.
In the end, the majority of the northern dissidents are granted South Korean citizenship.
More than 30,000 North Koreans have crossed the border illegally since the Korean war ended in 1953.
Most of them escalate towards China, which has the longest border towards North Korea, and it’s easier to cross than the strictly protected Demilitarized zone (DMZ) between the two Koreas.
The dissidents are received by China as an illegal immigration and not as a refugee, and is often forcibly returned.
Relations between the North and the South, which are still practically at war, have improved significantly in recent months.
Earlier this month, the leaders of the two countries met in Pyongyang, focusing on stalled nuclear disarmament negotiations.
